#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c24da7ef-0070-5aae-bade-c82ea56476ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9845" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x$. Its derivative is given by\n\n$y'(x) = x^4 (\\cos x)^x \\left(5 + g(x) \\right)$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." :question/worked-solution "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{5}) + \\ln ((\\cos x)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 5\\ln x + x\\ln (\\cos x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(5\\ln x + x\\ln (\\cos x)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= \\frac{5}{x} + (x)^{′} \\cdot \\ln (\\cos x) + x \\cdot (\\ln (\\cos x))^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) + x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x {:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x) \\\\\n&= x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x} + (x\\ln (\\cos x))/(x) - \\frac{x^{2}\\tan x}{x}) \\\\\n&= x^{4}(\\cos x)^{x}(5 + x ln ⁡ (cos ⁡ x) - x 2 tan ⁡ x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = x \\ln (\\cos x) - x^2 \\tan x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}$. The derivative of this function is given by\n$y' = -\\dfrac{g(x)(x+3)^2}{(x+5)^7}$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." :question/worked-solution "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (((x + 3)^{3})/((x + 5)^{6})) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((x + 3)^{3}) - \\ln ((x + 5)^{6}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 3\\ln (x + 3) - 6\\ln (x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(3\\ln (x + 3) - 6\\ln (x + 5)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x + 3} - 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x + 5} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{3}{x + 3} - \\frac{6}{x + 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{3}{x + 3} - \\frac{6}{x + 5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= ((x + 3)^{3})/((x + 5)^{6})(\\frac{3}{x + 3} - \\frac{6}{x + 5}) \\\\\n&= ((x + 3)^{3})/((x + 5)^{6})((3(x + 5) - 6(x + 3))/((x + 3)(x + 5))) \\\\\n&= ((x + 3)^{3})/((x + 5)^{6})((-3x - 3)/((x + 3)(x + 5))) \\\\\n&= - (3 (x + 1)(x + 3)^{2})/((x + 5)^{7})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{3(x+1)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}$. The derivative of this function is given by\n$y' = \\dfrac{g(x)}{3\\sqrt[3]{x(x^5+1)^4}}$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{5} + 1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}}{\\sqrt[3]{x^{5} + 1}} \\\\\n&= (x^{2/3})/((x^{5} + 1)^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((x^{2/3})/((x^{5} + 1)^{1/3})) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{2/3}) - \\ln ((x^{5} + 1)^{1/3}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\frac{2}{3}\\ln x - \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (x^{5} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2}{3}\\ln x - \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (x^{5} + 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^{5} + 1} \\cdot (5x^{4}) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{5x^{4}}{x^{5} + 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{5x^{4}}{x^{5} + 1}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{5} + 1}}(\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{5x^{4}}{x^{5} + 1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{5} + 1}}((2(x^{5} + 1) - 5x^{5})/(x(x^{5} + 1))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{5} + 1}}((-3x^{5} + 2)/(x(x^{5} + 1))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{5} + 1}}((-3x^{5} + 2)/(\\sqrt[3]{x^{3}(x^{5} + 1)^{3}})) \\\\\n&= (-3 x 5 + 2)/(3\\sqrt[3]{x(x^{5} + 1)^{4}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-3x^5+2}\n$$"}]}
